  "bundle": [
    {
      "path": "cordis.patch.yml",
      "text": "# dsh-search-order — composition patch.\n#\n# 1. Insert the plugin row. It registers the ordered dispatcher\n#    (`dsh-search-order`, the seam) plus every adapter this plugin owns:\n#    DeepSeek official search (three depth instances), DeepSeek Messages,\n#    Exa, Tavily, Perplexity and Brave Search. Keys and parameters for those\n#    adapters live in the plugin configuration and are editable from the card.\n# 2. Point the web seam at the dispatcher. The default chain is\n#    `['deepseek-search-api-text', 'dsh-search-order-messages']`, i.e. DeepSeek\n#    official search then Messages, until the user reorders anything in the card.\n#    `fetchProvider: http` is written explicitly on purpose: an `- id: web`\n#    override replaces the whole config block, and omitting it makes fetch\n#    provider selection ambiguous (`http` vs `deepseek-fetch-api`), which\n#    breaks `web_fetch`.\n# 3. Re-enable `tool-web` (the shipped web profile disables it). Fetch stays on,\n#    so the stock local-HTTP fetch provider serves `web_fetch`. The search budget\n#    covers the default chain's worst case (20s official + 60s Messages = 80s);\n#    if you raise a per-source timeout, keep their SUM inside this budget or the\n#    tool deadline will cut the chain short before the last source runs.\n\n- insert:\n    - id: dsh-search-order\n      name: dsh-search-order\n\n- id: web\n  config:\n    searchProvider: dsh-search-order\n    fetchProvider: http\n\n- id: tool-web\n  disabled: false\n  config:\n    fetch: true\n    searchTimeoutMs: 120000\n"
    }
  ],
